      "3 to General Scowcroft. Let me take this opportunity to restate our policy concerning the Arab-Israeli dispute and the peace process. This Administration is prepared to assist the parties toward attainment of a just and durable peace. Such a peace should come from direct negotiations among the parties based on United Nations Security Council resolutions 242 and 338, inv",
      "in the area covered by each paper-giver. The Conference is not an attempt at devising future security structures in the Middle East, between the Middle East and the rest of the international community etc. However, the lessons drawn from the conference may help in such efforts. DRAFT AGENDA (*) I. THE PRE-INVASION PERIOD (February - 2 August 1990) (a) The Iraqi-Kuwaiti",
      "tion will not allow Iraq to deflect attention from its illegal invasion of Kuwait by claiming there is a linkage to the Arab-Israeli conflict. The international community, including numerous Arab and Islamic nations, shares this view. The United States Government will continue to work diligently for the peaceful resolution of these two distinctly different but sensitive",
      "ou for your letter of July 23 and for sending me your document, Framework for a Public Peace Process: Toward a Peaceful Israeli-Palestinian Relationship. Please know that this administration will continue to do all that it can to bring peace and its benefits to Israel and all the peoples of the Middle East. Sincerely, NTHood Richard N. Haass Special Assistant to the President"
